# Stack-trace formatting: Python frames are reported with the class
# name scaffold (`<pycode>`) rather than the JVM-shaped class name,
# and method names are unsanitized (`bar_1` rather than `bar$1`). The
# structural fix is to teach `java.lang.StackTrace` to look up the
# bundle class by qualname and use its `_scpy_jvm_name`, plus a
# `_scpy_d`-aware reverse for method `co_name`s. Tracked separately
# from the getName/toString fix already landed in this item.
i3006b.scala                                # stack-trace-method-name (decoded `bar_1` instead of `bar$1`; reverse of `$ → _` for `co_name` not yet implemented)
i2738.scala                                 # stack-trace-class-name (declaringClass is `<pycode>`; reverse-lookup via `_scpy_class_registry` not implemented)
i3340.scala                                 # stack-trace-class-name (Throwable.getStackTrace reports Python qualnames)
i4947.scala                                 # stack-trace-class-name (Throwable.getStackTrace reports Python qualnames)
i4947a2.scala                               # stack-trace-class-name (Throwable.getStackTrace reports Python qualnames)
i4947b                                      # separate-compilation-resolution (dir-test compiles Lib_1 then Test_2; Test_2 fails to resolve `Lib_` because the second compile re-runs typer without the first's emitted .pyir on classpath)
assert-stack.scala                          # stack-trace-class-name (assertFailed stack reports `<lambda>`/`assertFailed` without owner)
# Tuple specialization: `Tuple2$mcII$sp` is the JVM specialization
# variant for `(Int, Int)`. The -scalapy backend does not emit the
# `mcII$sp` specialized class, so `getClass.getName` reports the
# generic `scala.Tuple2`. Structural to specialization lowering, not
# in item 12 scope.
i11114.scala                                # tuple-specialization-not-emitted
