# instead of `ForkJoinPool`. Removed from excludelist.

# --- Wave 5 item 05 (notes/wave5-worklist/05-assert-genuine-semantic.md) -
# Genuine Scala-vs-Python semantic gaps. The NaN equality sub-shape was
# fixed in `library-py BoxesRunTime.equals` (skip the `eq` shortcut for
# Python `float`s so generic `==` honours IEEE-754 `NaN != NaN`); the
# fixtures below remain blocked by separate, deeper structural gaps that
# fall outside this item's surgical scope.

# nan-instance-method-dispatch: resolved by Wave 6 item 01b — codegen
# intercept on boxed-primitive instance methods (Object/AnyRef/Number/
# boxed-prim receivers) routes `isNaN__Z`/`isInfinite__Z`/`*Value__*`
# calls through `_scpy_Double_*` / `_scpy_Boolean_*` runtime helpers
# that dispatch on `isinstance(x, float|int|bool)` and fall through to
# the encoded method on real ported boxes.
# (Entries `blame_eye_triple_eee-{double,float}.scala` removed.)

# python-lambda-class-identity-not-supported: Scala `() => x` lifts to a
# distinct synthetic class on the JVM; LambdaMetafactory may share the
# class for identical literals, but two lambdas with different captures
# always have distinct `getClass`. Python closures and SAM bridges all
# share `<class 'function'>` (or `_scpy_FnN`), so `lam1.getClass ==
# lam2.getClass` collapses to `true` regardless of the source. Without
# synthesizing a wrapper class per lambda site (which fights closure
# capture identity and Python serialization), this is a fundamental
# JVM-vs-Python semantic gap.
getclass.scala                              # python-lambda-class-identity-not-supported (asserts `f.getClass.toString` matches `class Test$$$Lambda$`; Python closure has no JVM-shaped class name)
lambda-serialization-security.scala         # python-lambda-class-identity-not-supported (asserts `reconstituted1.getClass == reconstituted2.getClass` after pickle round-trip; Python `function` class identity not preserved)

# explicit-boxed-number-not-stored: pylib `java.lang.Double(value)` /
# `java.lang.Float(value)` / `java.lang.Long(value)` constructors are
# empty bodies — the underlying primitive is recovered via
# `this.asInstanceOf[scala.Double]`, which works for the Predef
# `double2Double` implicit (boxing is identity in Python) but *not*
# for explicit `new java.lang.Double(d)`, which mints an `_scpy_Object`
# instance whose Python type is `java_lang_Double`, not `float`.
# `doubleValue()` on that instance returns the instance itself,
# which then propagates into `_scpy_float_to_str` and trips
# `math.isnan`. Structural fix is either to store the value in an
# instance attribute (e.g. `_scpy_value`) and have `doubleValue()`
# read it back, or to teach the runtime to unwrap `java_lang_*`
# proxies in primitive-typed call sites — both wider than item 05.
numbereq.scala                              # explicit-boxed-number-not-stored (`new java.lang.Double(x.toDouble)` paths; pylib boxes don't round-trip through `doubleValue()`)

